["<aside class=\"callout why\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Near Harrison you hunt the coast deer, not the interior mule deer. Name it in half a second and you know the rules, the habits and the shot.</p></aside><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-buck.jpg\" alt=\"Columbian black tailed buck in the Dungeness River, Washington, a doe at left. Red brown coat, dark tail, forked antlers like a mule deer.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Columbian black tailed buck in the Dungeness River, Washington, a doe at left. Red brown coat, dark tail, forked antlers like a mule deer. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Philip Cohen, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbian_Black-tailed_Deer_buck.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>6 chapters. This one: who the black tailed deer is and how to tell it apart.</li></ul><ul><li>Same species as the mule deer, smaller, darker, a forest deer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 345\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Six chapters: 1 meet the deer and tell it apart, 2 where they live season by season, 3 senses and the daily pattern, 4 the rut, 5 how to hunt them, 6 shot, recovery and meat in the wet. Chapters 1, 5 and 6 are marked to read first for a Harrison hunt.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"345\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Black tailed deer: 6 chapters</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"52\" width=\"372\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#FF6A13\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"36\" cy=\"70\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#FF6A13\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"36\" y=\"75\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">1</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"75\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Meet the deer and tell it apart</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"96\" width=\"372\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"36\" cy=\"114\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"36\" y=\"119\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">2</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"119\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Where they live, season by season</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"140\" width=\"372\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"36\" cy=\"158\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"36\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">3</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Senses and the daily pattern</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"184\" width=\"372\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"36\" cy=\"202\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"36\" y=\"207\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">4</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"207\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">The rut: timing and behaviour</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"228\" width=\"372\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#FF6A13\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"36\" cy=\"246\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#FF6A13\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"36\" y=\"251\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">5</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"251\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">How to hunt them</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"272\" width=\"372\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#FF6A13\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"36\" cy=\"290\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#FF6A13\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"36\" y=\"295\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">6</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"295\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Shot, recovery and meat in the wet</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"330\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Orange: read these 3 first for a Harrison hunt (Tip)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what the 6 chapters cover</summary><ol><li>Meet the deer (this one): ID, look alikes, antlers by age, size, numbers near Harrison.</li><li><a href=\"#/s/mc-black-tailed-deer-02\">Where they live, season by season</a>: spring cutblocks, summer high, winter old growth, the Harrison winter range.</li><li><a href=\"#/s/mc-black-tailed-deer-03\">Senses and the day</a>: dawn and dusk, beds, cover, rain.</li><li><a href=\"#/s/mc-black-tailed-deer-04\">The rut</a>: timing against your season, the tending bond.</li><li><a href=\"#/s/mc-black-tailed-deer-05\">How to hunt them</a>: still hunting, cutblock edges, partner work, rain days.</li><li><a href=\"#/s/mc-black-tailed-deer-06\">Shot, recovery and meat in the wet</a>: placement, tracking before the rain, meat cool and dry.</li><li>Flashcards: the \"Black tailed deer Masterclass\" deck.</li><li>Every fact has a certainty % (95 to 100 read in an official source; 80 to 94 official with some reading; 60 to 79 good secondary source). Under 60% is labelled Tip or <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Most biology here was measured on Vancouver Island and the south coast, not at Harrison itself. Where that matters, the line says so.</li><li>The interior cousin: <a href=\"#/s/mc-mule-deer-01\">Mule deer Masterclass</a>.</li></ol></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 305\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side view of a black tailed deer buck with labels: wide tail dark on top, small white rump patch, ears shorter than a mule deer, forked antlers, a smaller and darker body. Tap the parts to learn each one.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"305\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Black tailed deer: 4 marks</text>\n<ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"170\" rx=\"92\" ry=\"44\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M262 150 L292 92 L314 98 L292 165 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"318\" cy=\"92\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"15\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"340\" cy=\"96\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#17201A\"/><rect x=\"130\" y=\"200\" width=\"13\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"160\" y=\"202\" width=\"13\" height=\"68\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"236\" y=\"200\" width=\"13\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"262\" y=\"198\" width=\"13\" height=\"72\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><g data-tip=\"Antlers|Fork like a mule deer. Yearlings are almost always spikes (90%).\" data-tip-dot=\"306,50\"><path d=\"M308 80 L306 52 M306 52 L296 36 M306 52 L318 38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#17201A\" stroke-width=\"4\"/></g><g data-tip=\"Ears|About 3 cm shorter than a mule deer (90%).\" data-tip-dot=\"300,66\"><ellipse cx=\"300\" cy=\"70\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" transform=\"rotate(-25 300 70)\"/></g><g data-tip=\"Body|Smaller and slightly darker than a mule deer (90%).\" data-tip-dot=\"190,170\"><rect x=\"150\" y=\"150\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"none\"/></g><g data-tip=\"Rump patch|Small white rump patch (90%).\" data-tip-dot=\"120,160\"><ellipse cx=\"116\" cy=\"160\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"18\" fill=\"#f4efe2\"/></g><g data-tip=\"Tail|Wide. Dark brown or black on top for most of its length (90%). Raised in alarm, it shows a broad white underside (80%).\" data-tip-dot=\"107,166\"><rect x=\"100\" y=\"146\" width=\"14\" height=\"40\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#17201A\"/></g><text x=\"20\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Wide dark tail</text><line x1=\"64\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"176\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Shorter ears</text><line x1=\"260\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"292\" y2=\"66\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Small rump patch</text><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"278\" x2=\"112\" y2=\"178\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"390\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Smaller, darker body</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Tail: wide, dark brown or black on top for most of its length <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Small white rump patch, ears about 3 cm shorter than a mule deer's <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the full ID list</summary><ul><li><strong>Tail:</strong> dark brown or black for most of its length, not just the tip (90%, BC deer account 2000). WDFW (Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife): broad, dark brown on the back, black near the tip <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Alarm:</strong> a raised tail shows the broad white underside (80%, WDFW).</li><li><strong>Rump:</strong> a small white rump patch; the mule deer's is large <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li><strong>Ears:</strong> smaller than a mule deer's, about 3 cm shorter (90%, Nyberg and Janz 1990).</li><li><strong>Body and coat:</strong> smaller and slightly darker than a mule deer; a browner winter coat; smaller glands on the hock <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li><strong>Antlers:</strong> branch like a mule deer's: each beam forks, then forks again <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Chapter 1, \"Antlers by age\", has the detail.</li><li>Sitka black tails (north coast, Haida Gwaii) are smaller and darker again. Not near Harrison <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-doe.jpg\" alt=\"Black tailed doe: the whole top of the tail is dark.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black tailed doe: the whole top of the tail is dark. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Walter Siegmund (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odocoileus_hemionus_5432.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-rear.jpg\" alt=\"Mule deer: thin white tail, black tip only.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Mule deer: thin white tail, black tip only. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Buck_(15382465555).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-buck-flag.jpg\" alt=\"White tailed deer: wide tail, white flag up.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>White tailed deer: wide tail, white flag up. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: ForestWander, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Whitetail-buck-running-snow_-_West_Virginia_-_ForestWander.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-bedded.jpg\" alt=\"Young black tailed buck bedded: dark face, short spikes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young black tailed buck bedded: dark face, short spikes. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Buiobuione, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-tailed_deer_-_Odocoileus_hemionus_columbianus_-_05.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 345\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four checks before a shot in Region 2. 1 a deer, not an elk: elk have no general season in Region 2. 2 tail dark on top: a white flag tail is a white tailed deer, with no season. 3 visible bony antlers: that is a buck, and a spike counts. 4 a safe and legal spot: backstop, no road, no house, legal light. Any no or not sure: no shot.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"345\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Legal or not? 4 checks</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"48\" width=\"372\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"76\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"81\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">1</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Deer, not elk?</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Elk: no general season in Region 2</text><path d=\"M200 104 L200 114\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"14\" y=\"114\" width=\"372\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#FF6A13\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"142\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#FF6A13\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"147\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">2</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Tail dark on top?</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"159\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">White flag tail = white tail: NO season</text><path d=\"M200 170 L200 180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"14\" y=\"180\" width=\"372\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"208\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"213\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">3</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"204\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Visible bony antlers?</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"225\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">That is a buck. A spike counts</text><path d=\"M200 236 L200 246\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"14\" y=\"246\" width=\"372\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"274\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"279\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">4</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Safe and legal spot?</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"291\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Backstop, no road, no house, legal light</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"330\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#FF6A13\">Any no or not sure: no shot</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>White tailed deer: no season anywhere in Region 2 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>Not sure? No shot (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the look alike table</summary><aside class=\"callout warn\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Look alike</th><th>Tell it by</th><th>Legal point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mule deer or hybrid</td><td>Big white rump, thin white tail with a black tip <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></td><td>One species in law: \"mule (black-tailed) deer\" (99%, page 27). Same licence (99%, page 8)</td></tr><tr><td>White tailed deer</td><td>Wide brown tail, white flag; tines rise off one main beam <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>No season in Region 2 (99%, pages 14 and 27). Some live at Herrling Island near Hope (99%, page 27)</td></tr><tr><td>Elk</td><td>Much bigger, dark neck, pale yellow rump <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>No Region 2 general season row (99%, page 28)</td></tr><tr><td>Doe or fawn</td><td>No visible antlers</td><td>No antlerless season in MUs (Management Units) 2-8, 2-18, 2-19 (99%, page 28)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Coast and interior deer interbreed where their ranges meet, so some valley deer look mixed (90%, BC deer account). In law they are one species, so a hybrid buck uses the same licence (85%, my reading).</li><li>More rules: <a href=\"#/s/local-harrison-deer\">Your Harrison deer hunt</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-2\">Rule Book: Region 2</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 325\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Black tailed buck antlers by age. 1.5 years: spikes, about 90% of yearlings. 2.5 years: most forked, 2 points a side. 3.5 years and older: 2, 3 or 4 points a side. All are bucks, legal in MUs 2-8, 2-18 and 2-19.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"325\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Antlers by age (a rough guide)</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"46\" width=\"120\" height=\"196\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"196\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"140\" y=\"46\" width=\"120\" height=\"196\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"196\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"46\" width=\"120\" height=\"196\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"330\" cy=\"196\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M60 184 L56 110 M80 184 L84 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#17201A\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M190 184 L184 130 L170 96 M184 130 L196 100 M210 184 L216 130 L230 96 M216 130 L204 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#17201A\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M320 184 L310 130 L292 92 M310 130 L322 98 M300 112 L288 120 M314 168 L304 162 M340 184 L350 130 L368 92 M350 130 L338 98 M360 112 L372 120 M346 168 L356 162\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#17201A\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">1.5 years</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Spikes</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">about 90%</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">2.5 years</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Most forked,</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">2 points a side</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">3.5 years +</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">2, 3 or 4</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">points a side</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">All are bucks: legal in 2-8, 2-18, 2-19 (99%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Yearlings: about 90% are spikes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>A spike is a buck. Legal in 2-8, 2-18, 2-19 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-victoria.jpg\" alt=\"Young buck, Victoria: wide dark tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young buck, Victoria: wide dark tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Gordon Leggett, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025-04-13_01_Black-tailed_deer_(Odocoileus_hemionus_columbianus)_%E2%80%93_Victoria_BC_Canada.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/bg-md-blacktail-vi.jpg\" alt=\"Young buck, Vancouver Island: short velvet antlers.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young buck, Vancouver Island: short velvet antlers. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Buiobuione, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-tailed_deer_in_Vancouver_Island_British_Columbia_Canada_06.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-buck-velvet.jpg\" alt=\"Older buck in velvet: forks on forks.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Older buck in velvet: forks on forks. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbian_black-tailed_deer_(Odocoileus_hemionus_columbianus)_male_Mt_Rainier.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: antlers, age and the law</summary><aside class=\"callout warn\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><ul><li>South coast data (90%, Nyberg and Janz 1990):<ul><li>Male fawns grow no antlers; you may see the bumps (pedicels) up close.</li><li>Yearlings: about 90% are spikes with long single point antlers; about 10% have one more point a side.</li><li>By 2.5 years most bucks have forks, 2 points a side.</li><li>By 4.5 years, on good range, usually 3 or more points per antler. On poor range many adult bucks only grow forks.</li></ul></li><li>BC deer account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: yearlings almost always spikes; two year olds mostly small 2 points, sometimes spikes; 3 years and older 2, 3 or 4 points a side. Points are \"not a reliable way to determine the age\".</li><li>The law: a buck is a deer \"bearing visible bony antlers\" (99%, page 4). The rows for MUs 2-8, 2-18 and 2-19 say Bucks, not 4 point bucks (99%, page 28). So a spike is a legal buck there (95%, my reading).</li><li>Harvested bucks on Vancouver Island (Campbell River check, 1976 to 1980): average age 2.5 years; only 4% older than 5.5 (90%, Nyberg and Janz).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 325\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Weight ranges in kilograms. Mule deer buck 68 to 113. Black tailed buck 48 to 90. Black tailed doe 40 to 65. South coast doe in summer and fall 35 to 45. Chest height about 57 cm on Vancouver Island.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"325\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Smaller than a mule deer</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Mule deer buck</text><rect x=\"280.33333333333337\" y=\"58\" width=\"86.25\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"276.33333333333337\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">68</text><text x=\"370.58333333333337\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">113</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Black tail buck</text><rect x=\"242.0\" y=\"108\" width=\"80.5\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"238.0\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">48</text><text x=\"326.5\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">90</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Black tail doe</text><rect x=\"226.66666666666669\" y=\"158\" width=\"47.91666666666663\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"222.66666666666669\" y=\"206\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">40</text><text x=\"278.5833333333333\" y=\"206\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">65</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Coast doe, summer</text><rect x=\"217.08333333333331\" y=\"208\" width=\"19.166666666666686\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c9d3c0\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"213.08333333333331\" y=\"256\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">35</text><text x=\"240.25\" y=\"256\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">45</text><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"262\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#17201A\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"265\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">kilograms</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Chest height about 57 cm (Vancouver Island)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Buck 48 to 90 kg, doe 40 to 65 kg <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>The vital zone is small. Short, sure shots (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: size details and the Fraser Valley</summary><ul><li>BC deer account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: adult males in good condition about 48 to 90 kg, females 40 to 65 kg. Mule deer bucks 68 to 113 kg.</li><li>South coast (90%, Nyberg and Janz 1990): adult does 35 to 45 kg in summer and fall, 50 to 60 kg on excellent range. Males 50 to 110 kg, \"with the largest ones coming from the fertile lower Fraser Valley\" and from migratory deer that feed high in summer.</li><li>WDFW <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>: bucks 140 to 200 lb (64 to 91 kg), does 90 to 130 lb (41 to 59 kg).</li><li>Chest height of an adult on Vancouver Island: about 57 cm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. That is knee to hip on most adults (Tip).</li><li>Bucks are heaviest about mid November to early December, then lose 20 to 25% over most winters <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Chapter 4 has the rut year.</li><li>Pack out: a field dressed buck is still a load. Chapter 6.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 404\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Map of BC, zones schematic. The Coast Range splits the deer. West of it: Columbian black tailed deer, including Vancouver Island and the Fraser Valley. North coast and Haida Gwaii: Sitka black tailed deer, introduced to Haida Gwaii. East of the Coast Range: Rocky Mountain mule deer in the interior, including Kamloops. Hybrids occur where they meet.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"404\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Three races in BC (schematic)</text>\n<path d=\"M246.8 320.0 L378.9 320.0 L373.9 310.0 L369.5 304.2 L363.6 287.5 L356.3 272.5 L346.0 258.8 L331.3 242.5 L321.0 232.5 L314.4 222.5 L303.4 210.0 L291.6 200.0 L291.6 45.0 L11.7 45.0 L18.3 51.3 L34.5 72.5 L49.1 60.0 L63.8 50.0 L69.0 54.5 L81.5 65.0 L94.7 85.0 L110.9 110.0 L115.3 127.5 L130.0 140.0 L144.7 147.5 L143.9 162.5 L135.8 177.5 L140.3 187.5 L146.1 202.5 L156.4 220.0 L166.7 230.0 L172.6 245.0 L177.0 265.0 L188.7 272.5 L203.4 280.0 L218.1 290.0 L227.0 300.0 L238.7 308.8 L244.6 312.5 L246.8 320.0z\" fill=\"#ead9a0\"/>\n<path d=\"M271.0 320.0 L246.8 320.0 L244.6 312.5 L238.7 308.8 L227.0 300.0 L218.1 290.0 L203.4 280.0 L188.7 272.5 L177.0 265.0 L172.6 245.0 L166.7 230.0 L156.4 220.0 L146.1 202.5 L140.3 187.5 L135.8 177.5 L143.9 162.5 L144.7 147.5 L130.0 140.0 L141.7 135.0 L156.4 165.0 L169.6 190.0 L182.9 212.5 L197.6 235.0 L215.2 255.0 L238.7 272.5 L256.3 290.0 L271.0 311.3z\" fill=\"#bcd3b0\"/>\n<path d=\"M246.8 320.0 L378.9 320.0 L373.9 310.0 L369.5 304.2 L363.6 287.5 L356.3 272.5 L346.0 258.8 L331.3 242.5 L321.0 232.5 L314.4 222.5 L303.4 210.0 L291.6 200.0 L291.6 45.0 L11.7 45.0 L18.3 51.3 L34.5 72.5 L49.1 60.0 L63.8 50.0 L69.0 54.5 L81.5 65.0 L94.7 85.0 L110.9 110.0 L115.3 127.5 L130.0 140.0 L144.7 147.5 L143.9 162.5 L135.8 177.5 L140.3 187.5 L146.1 202.5 L156.4 220.0 L166.7 230.0 L172.6 245.0 L177.0 265.0 L188.7 272.5 L203.4 280.0 L218.1 290.0 L227.0 300.0 L238.7 308.8 L244.6 312.5 L246.8 320.0z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8a7a50\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n<path d=\"M168.2 275.0 L181.4 276.3 L194.6 282.5 L209.3 288.8 L218.1 300.0 L227.0 310.0 L234.3 316.3 L241.6 326.3 L241.6 336.3 L232.8 335.0 L221.1 330.0 L209.3 321.2 L197.6 310.0 L185.8 297.5 L174.0 286.2z\" fill=\"#bcd3b0\" stroke=\"#8a7a50\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<path d=\"M99.8 190.0 L119.7 191.3 L116.0 212.5 L127.0 235.0 L130.0 246.2 L121.1 238.8 L109.4 222.5 L99.1 205.0z\" fill=\"#7fae95\" stroke=\"#4d7a62\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<path d=\"M271.0 311.3 L256.3 290.0 L238.7 272.5 L215.2 255.0 L197.6 235.0 L182.9 212.5 L169.6 190.0 L156.4 165.0 L141.7 135.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<text x=\"250\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mule deer</text>\n<text x=\"250\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">interior</text>\n<circle cx=\"286.8\" cy=\"278.2\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<text x=\"295.8\" y=\"283.2\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#c2410c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" paint-order=\"stroke\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">Kamloops</text>\n<circle cx=\"257.8\" cy=\"316.8\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"159.355\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"186\" y2=\"101\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"159.355\" cy=\"170\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"132.0\" y=\"74.0\" width=\"108.1\" height=\"27.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"186\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Coast Range</text>\n<line x1=\"110.862\" y1=\"212.5\" x2=\"82\" y2=\"258\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"110.862\" cy=\"212.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"5.8\" y=\"258.0\" width=\"152.4\" height=\"45.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"82\" y=\"277\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sitka: Haida Gwaii</text>\n<text x=\"82\" y=\"294.5\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(introduced)</text>\n<line x1=\"209.316\" y1=\"302.5\" x2=\"112.31\" y2=\"329.5\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"209.316\" cy=\"302.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"27.7\" y=\"316.0\" width=\"84.6\" height=\"27.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#5b7a50\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"335\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Black tail</text>\n<line x1=\"257.809\" y1=\"316.75\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"352\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"257.809\" cy=\"316.75\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n<rect x=\"17.3\" y=\"352.0\" width=\"201.5\" height=\"27.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"118\" y=\"371\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2c5d86\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fraser Valley: black tails</text>\n<line x1=\"253.4\" y1=\"288.75\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"352\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"253.4\" cy=\"288.75\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<rect x=\"259.4\" y=\"352.0\" width=\"121.2\" height=\"45.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"320\" y=\"371\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hybrids where</text>\n<text x=\"320\" y=\"389\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">they meet</text>\n<g transform=\"translate(372 64)\"><circle r=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><path d=\"M0 -11 L5.9 7.0 L0 2.8 L-5.9 7.0z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text y=\"-18\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"3\" paint-order=\"stroke\">N</text></g>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Coast, west of the Coast and Cascade summits: black tails <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>The Fraser Valley is black tail country <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/bg-md-sitka-haida.jpg\" alt=\"Sitka black tailed doe, Haida Gwaii: smaller, redder.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Sitka black tailed doe, Haida Gwaii: smaller, redder. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Murray Foubister, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haida_Gwaii_(Queen_Charlotte_Islands)_-_Graham_Island_-_Sitka_Black-Tailed_Deer_(Odocoileus_hemionus_sitkensis)_-_(20939560023).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-buck.jpg\" alt=\"Columbian black tailed buck and doe, Washington.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Columbian black tailed buck and doe, Washington. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Philip Cohen, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbian_Black-tailed_Deer_buck.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: three races and numbers</summary><ul><li>One species, Odocoileus hemionus, three races in BC (90%, BC deer account): mule deer (interior), Columbian black tailed deer (coast, Vancouver Island, Fraser Valley, Sechelt Peninsula), Sitka black tailed deer (north coast, introduced to Haida Gwaii).</li><li>The Coast Range largely separates black tails and mule deer, mostly in winter. They interbreed where the ranges meet <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Black tails spread north from Washington as the ice left, 15,000 to 10,000 years ago. Long isolation made them \"a species in the making\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>BC black tails: 150,000 to 250,000 in recent decades (90%, BC deer account 2000).</li><li>Black tails swim well and live on most coastal islands <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 322\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Hunter days per deer taken, residents, 2020 to 2024. Region 2 average 13.5 days. MU 2-18 24.6 days. MU 2-19 27.5 days. MU 2-8 30.3 days. Survey estimates, computed by Hunt Mentor.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"322\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Harrison is harder than average</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Region 2</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"56\" width=\"89.1\" height=\"34\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"197.1\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">13.5 days</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">MU 2-18</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"108\" width=\"162.36\" height=\"34\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"270.36\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">24.6 days</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">MU 2-19</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"160\" width=\"181.5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"289.5\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">27.5 days</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">MU 2-8</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"212\" width=\"199.98\" height=\"34\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"307.98\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">30.3 days</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Hunter days per deer taken, 2020 to 2024</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"308\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Survey estimates, computed (90%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>2-18 (east side, Agassiz): about 25 hunter days per deer, all 5 years pooled <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Your plan's odds use the middle year: about 21.</li><li>Region 2 average: about 13 days. Plan several trips (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the numbers behind the chart</summary><ul><li>Source: BC Big Game Harvest Statistics, resident hunters, mule deer rows (black tails in Region 2), 2020 to 2024. Survey estimates. Hunt Mentor computed the totals <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Deer, 5 years</th><th>Hunter days</th><th>Days per deer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MU 2-18</td><td>216</td><td>5,304</td><td>24.6</td></tr><tr><td>MU 2-19</td><td>96</td><td>2,641</td><td>27.5</td></tr><tr><td>MU 2-8</td><td>115</td><td>3,488</td><td>30.3</td></tr><tr><td>Region 2</td><td>5,876</td><td>79,496</td><td>13.5</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>2024 alone: 2-18, 69 deer from 1,075 days; 2-19, 8 deer from 380 days <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Why harder? Not known from the data. Thick cover is one likely reason: the coastal handbook says abundant cover means hunting has little effect on coast deer numbers (90%, Nyberg and Janz). The rest is a guess (Tip).</li><li>MU map: <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-2\">Rule Book: Region 2</a>. Spots: the <a href=\"#/map\">Hunt Map</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three deer rumps from behind. Mule deer: big white rump, thin white tail with only a black tip. Black tailed deer: smaller rump, wide tail dark on top for its whole length. White tailed deer: wide brown tail, flipped up as a white flag when alarmed. Mule and black tailed deer are one species, two races.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Read the tail from behind</text><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"120\" rx=\"48\" ry=\"58\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"110\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"40\" fill=\"#f4efe2\"/><rect x=\"65\" y=\"92\" width=\"10\" height=\"52\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#f4efe2\"/><rect x=\"65\" y=\"132\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#23261f\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mule deer</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">thin, white,</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"248\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">black tip only</text><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"120\" rx=\"48\" ry=\"58\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"110\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"32\" fill=\"#f4efe2\"/><rect x=\"189\" y=\"88\" width=\"22\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#23261f\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Black tail</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">wide, dark on</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"248\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">top all the way</text><ellipse cx=\"330\" cy=\"120\" rx=\"48\" ry=\"58\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"316\" y=\"84\" width=\"28\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><path d=\"M316 84 L306 40 L354 40 L344 84 Z\" fill=\"#f4efe2\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"330\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">White tail</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">wide, brown,</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"248\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">white flag up</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"288\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mule and black tail: one species, two races (90%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><strong>Rhyme</strong> (Tip): \"Black on top, the whole way down: coast deer in a forest gown.\"</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 90, 2, 57: 90% of yearlings are spikes, forks by 2.5, chest 57 cm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law): \"Tail, then antlers, then the backstop. In that order, every time.\"</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Calling every Fraser Valley deer a mule deer and hunting it like open country.</li><li>Passing a spike because \"it is too small to be legal\". In 2-8, 2-18 and 2-19 a spike is a buck.</li><li>Shooting at a flag tail in the brush. White tails have no season in Region 2.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Next drive to Harrison at dusk: for every deer, say out loud \"tail, rump, antlers, buck or doe\".</p></aside>","<ul><li>BC deer account 2000, read directly <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Nyberg and Janz 1990, read directly from page images <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. WDFW deer page <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Synopsis pages 4, 8, 14, 27, 28 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>. BC harvest statistics <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Checked 2026-10-23.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source and link</summary><ul><li>BC Ministry of Environment, Lands and Parks (2000), <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/muledeer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Mule and Black-tailed Deer in British Columbia\"</a>, read directly 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Nyberg, J.B. and D.W. Janz, editors (1990), <a href=\"https://www.for.gov.bc.ca/hfd/pubs/docs/srs/Srs05.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Deer and Elk Habitats in Coastal Forests of Southern British Columbia\"</a>, BC Ministry of Forests Special Report Series 5, pages 33 to 37, read directly 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife, <a href=\"https://wdfw.wa.gov/species-habitats/living/species-facts/deer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Living with wildlife: deer</a>, read 2026-10-23 (80%; Washington, not BC).</li><li><a href=\"https://catalogue.data.gov.bc.ca/dataset/big-game-harvest-statistics-1976-to-2024\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Big Game Harvest Statistics 1976 to 2024</a>, via the app's harvest data, checked 2026-10-04 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, 2 October 2026 edition, pages 4, 8, 14, 27, 28, via <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-2\">Rule Book: Region 2</a> and <a href=\"#/s/local-harrison-deer\">Your Harrison deer hunt</a> <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul></details>"]